{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EPu1Yq48ppHESMaNHoVNYV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — solver-trace-ablation\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (NLP and Symbolic Reasoning track)\n5\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n6\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (2 条，精简自原始 5 条)\n9\t\n10\t### Claim 1: STA as Reward Design Methodology\n11\tSolver Trace Ablation (STA), grounded in the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) framework from mutation testing theory, provides a **general, solver-agnostic procedure** for deriving intensional verification rewards from solver execution semantics. For any solver+language pair, a 5-step derivation protocol (pilot → collect shortcuts → classify → map RIP → design check) produces domain-specific structural rewards. This is the first application of the RIP framework to RL reward design.\n12\t\n13\t**MVP 证据**: Full STA 在 FOL+Prover9 上提供连续 ISR（0.125-0.889），成功检测 GRPO 训练中的 reward hacking（ISR/PR 比从 64%→33%）。O(1) 近似（tautology-only check）信号过粗无效——重要阴性结果，证明了 full component ablation 的必要性。\n14\t\n15\t### Claim 2: Multi-Logic GRPO Empirical Findings\n16\tMulti-logic GRPO training across structurally different formal languages (FOL→Prover9, Prolog→SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB→Z3) exhibits measurable cross-logic transfer patterns and logic-type-specific reward hacking modes. STA-derived rewards detect and quantify these phenomena.\n17\t\n18\t**MVP 证据**: 待验证（FOL 单逻辑已验证，multi-logic 需 research 阶段实验）。\n19\t\n20\t## Story Spine\n21\t\n22\t- **Problem**: GRPO with solver-verified rewards trains formalizers that achieve high execution accuracy but produce structurally degenerate outputs (reward hacking). Existing structural reward designs are ad-hoc heuristics with no principled derivation.\n23\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mellgren et al. (2025) hand-craft Prolog-specific structure penalties. EVOM (2026) demonstrates cross-solver GRPO transfer but uses scalar execution rewards only. ExecVerify/EGCA derive trace-level rewards for code, not formal logic. No work provides a general derivation procedure for structural rewards from solver semantics.\n24\t- **Our framing**: We adapt the RIP framework from mutation testing — a component is \"necessary\" iff its ablation changes the solver's output — to derive intensional verification rewards. STA fills the **dense + automatic** cell in the ORM-PRM reward taxonomy (ORM=sparse+automatic, PRM=dense+manual, STA=dense+automatic via solver semantics). This positions STA not as \"a new algorithm\" but as \"the first systematic transfer of mutation testing methodology to RL reward design.\"\n25\t- **Headline result template**: \"On FOLIO/ProofWriter/LogiQA, STA-rewarded GRPO achieves <X>% formalization accuracy with <Y>% ISR, compared to <Z>% accuracy / <W>% ISR for execution-only GRPO — maintaining accuracy while detecting and reducing structural degeneracy.\"\n26\t\n27\t## Venue Fit Risks\n28\t\n29\t- **EMNLP fit**: Core contribution is neurosymbolic (NLP + formal logic), directly in the NLP and Symbolic Reasoning track. Formal verification + RL reward design is highly relevant.\n30\t- **\"Old wine\" risk**: STA's component ablation resembles mutation testing. Mitigation: explicitly acknowledge the RIP origin, claim novelty at the methodology transfer level + empirical findings, not at the algorithm level.\n31\t- **Scope breadth risk**: Original proposal had 5 contributions — trimmed to 2 core (STA methodology + empirical findings). MLSR framework, hacking taxonomy, STA-IPT duality are secondary analysis within these 2 contributions.\n32\t- **Single-logic risk**: If only FOL results are available, paper may feel narrow. Mitigation: at minimum include Prolog as second logic type; SMT-LIB as stretch goal.\n33\t\n34\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n35\t\n36\t1. **\"STA is just mutation testing applied to FOL\"** — Response: yes, that's the point. The contribution is the systematic transfer + empirical validation that it works as RL reward, not algorithmic novelty. The 5-step derivation protocol is the transferable methodology.\n37\t2. **\"O(1) approximation doesn't work\"** — Response: this is a feature, not a bug. We report it as a principled negative result showing that domain-specific hacking modes require corresponding ablation granularity.\n38\t3. **\"Low prove rate limits GRPO signal\"** — Response: addressed by K=8+ and difficulty-based data selection. Prove rate increases during training (12%→15-20%).\n39\t4. **\"ISR/PR ratio declining even with STA reward\"** — Response: 100-step pilot is too short; full runs with curriculum will show whether STA prevents or just detects degradation. Even detection alone is a contribution (monitoring tool).\n40\t5. **\"Only tested on one/two logic types\"** — Response: STA derivation protocol is solver-agnostic by design; instantiations for additional logic types follow the same 5-step procedure.\n41\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78583d99-c2f9-4acd-a60c-521218e1ab09","uuid":"654238ac-b6fa-4fe1-b365-8d348f524f28","timestamp":"2026-05-13T22:14:11.778Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T215750/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — solver-trace-ablation\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (NLP and Symbolic Reasoning track)\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026\n\n## Core Claims (2 条，精简自原始 5 条)\n\n### Claim 1: STA as Reward Design Methodology\nSolver Trace Ablation (STA), grounded in the Reachability-Infection-Propagation (RIP) framework from mutation testing theory, provides a **general, solver-agnostic procedure** for deriving intensional verification rewards from solver execution semantics. For any solver+language pair, a 5-step derivation protocol (pilot → collect shortcuts → classify → map RIP → design check) produces domain-specific structural rewards. This is the first application of the RIP framework to RL reward design.\n\n**MVP 证据**: Full STA 在 FOL+Prover9 上提供连续 ISR（0.125-0.889），成功检测 GRPO 训练中的 reward hacking（ISR/PR 比从 64%→33%）。O(1) 近似（tautology-only check）信号过粗无效——重要阴性结果，证明了 full component ablation 的必要性。\n\n### Claim 2: Multi-Logic GRPO Empirical Findings\nMulti-logic GRPO training across structurally different formal languages (FOL→Prover9, Prolog→SWI-Prolog, SMT-LIB→Z3) exhibits measurable cross-logic transfer patterns and logic-type-specific reward hacking modes. STA-derived rewards detect and quantify these phenomena.\n\n**MVP 证据**: 待验证（FOL 单逻辑已验证，multi-logic 需 research 阶段实验）。\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: GRPO with solver-verified rewards trains formalizers that achieve high execution accuracy but produce structurally degenerate outputs (reward hacking). Existing structural reward designs are ad-hoc heuristics with no principled derivation.\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mellgren et al. (2025) hand-craft Prolog-specific structure penalties. EVOM (2026) demonstrates cross-solver GRPO transfer but uses scalar execution rewards only. ExecVerify/EGCA derive trace-level rewards for code, not formal logic. No work provides a general derivation procedure for structural rewards from solver semantics.\n- **Our framing**: We adapt the RIP framework from mutation testing — a component is \"necessary\" iff its ablation changes the solver's output — to derive intensional verification rewards. STA fills the **dense + automatic** cell in the ORM-PRM reward taxonomy (ORM=sparse+automatic, PRM=dense+manual, STA=dense+automatic via solver semantics). This positions STA not as \"a new algorithm\" but as \"the first systematic transfer of mutation testing methodology to RL reward design.\"\n- **Headline result template**: \"On FOLIO/ProofWriter/LogiQA, STA-rewarded GRPO achieves <X>% formalization accuracy with <Y>% ISR, compared to <Z>% accuracy / <W>% ISR for execution-only GRPO — maintaining accuracy while detecting and reducing structural degeneracy.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **EMNLP fit**: Core contribution is neurosymbolic (NLP + formal logic), directly in the NLP and Symbolic Reasoning track. Formal verification + RL reward design is highly relevant.\n- **\"Old wine\" risk**: STA's component ablation resembles mutation testing. Mitigation: explicitly acknowledge the RIP origin, claim novelty at the methodology transfer level + empirical findings, not at the algorithm level.\n- **Scope breadth risk**: Original proposal had 5 contributions — trimmed to 2 core (STA methodology + empirical findings). MLSR framework, hacking taxonomy, STA-IPT duality are secondary analysis within these 2 contributions.\n- **Single-logic risk**: If only FOL results are available, paper may feel narrow. Mitigation: at minimum include Prolog as second logic type; SMT-LIB as stretch goal.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. **\"STA is just mutation testing applied to FOL\"** — Response: yes, that's the point. The contribution is the systematic transfer + empirical validation that it works as RL reward, not algorithmic novelty. The 5-step derivation protocol is the transferable methodology.\n2. **\"O(1) approximation doesn't work\"** — Response: this is a feature, not a bug. We report it as a principled negative result showing that domain-specific hacking modes require corresponding ablation granularity.\n3. **\"Low prove rate limits GRPO signal\"** — Response: addressed by K=8+ and difficulty-based data selection. Prove rate increases during training (12%→15-20%).\n4. **\"ISR/PR ratio declining even with STA reward\"** — Response: 100-step pilot is too short; full runs with curriculum will show whether STA prevents or just detects degradation. Even detection alone is a contribution (monitoring tool).\n5. **\"Only tested on one/two logic types\"** — Response: STA derivation protocol is solver-agnostic by design; instantiations for additional logic types follow the same 5-step procedure.\n","numLines":41,"startLine":1,"totalLines":41}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R4boHxYz1HCEzrQ61h6aTC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"FOL Exec-Only 500-Step Baseline\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"FOL single-logic GRPO, execution-only reward, 500 steps, K=8, 3 seeds. Establishes the structural degradation trajectory that STA is designed to prevent.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"prove_rate_mean\": 0.28,\n7\t      \"prove_rate_std\": 0.03,\n8\t      \"prove_rate_first100\": 0.18,\n9\t      \"prove_rate_last100\": 0.33,\n10\t      \"mean_isr_overall\": 0.11,\n11\t      \"mean_isr_last100\": 0.09,\n12\t      \"isr_pr_ratio_overall\": 0.39,\n13\t      \"isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.35,\n14\t      \"isr_pr_ratio_first100\": 0.92,\n15\t      \"parse_rate\": 1.0,\n16\t      \"training_minutes\": 79,\n17\t      \"step_time_s\": 9.5\n18\t    },\n19\t    \"tables\": [\n20\t      {\n21\t        \"name\": \"Table 2a: Exec-Only Training Dynamics (3-seed mean ± std)\",\n22\t        \"columns\": [\n23\t          \"step_window\",\n24\t          \"prove_rate\",\n25\t          \"mean_isr\",\n26\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n27\t          \"reward_mean\"\n28\t        ],\n29\t        \"rows\": [\n30\t          [\n31\t            \"1-100\",\n32\t            \"0.18 ± 0.02\",\n33\t            \"0.16 ± 0.02\",\n34\t            \"0.92 ± 0.05\",\n35\t            \"0.18 ± 0.02\"\n36\t          ],\n37\t          [\n38\t            \"101-200\",\n39\t            \"0.22 ± 0.02\",\n40\t            \"0.16 ± 0.03\",\n41\t            \"0.73 ± 0.08\",\n42\t            \"0.22 ± 0.02\"\n43\t          ],\n44\t          [\n45\t            \"201-300\",\n46\t            \"0.25 ± 0.03\",\n47\t            \"0.13 ± 0.03\",\n48\t            \"0.52 ± 0.07\",\n49\t            \"0.25 ± 0.03\"\n50\t          ],\n51\t          [\n52\t            \"301-400\",\n53\t            \"0.27 ± 0.03\",\n54\t            \"0.11 ± 0.02\",\n55\t            \"0.41 ± 0.06\",\n56\t            \"0.27 ± 0.03\"\n57\t          ],\n58\t          [\n59\t            \"401-500\",\n60\t            \"0.33 ± 0.04\",\n61\t            \"0.09 ± 0.02\",\n62\t            \"0.35 ± 0.05\",\n63\t            \"0.33 ± 0.04\"\n64\t          ]\n65\t        ]\n66\t      }\n67\t    ],\n68\t    \"figures\": [],\n69\t    \"analysis\": \"The exec-only trajectory confirms the core problem statement: execution-only GRPO optimizes prove_rate effectively (+133% from baseline) but introduces structural degeneracy that accelerates after step ~150. The declining isr_pr_ratio is the paper's primary diagnostic signal. Notably, prove_rate continues improving even as ISR declines, meaning standard evaluation (accuracy-only) would rate this as a successful training run. Only STA-derived ISR reveals the hidden failure mode. This motivates the need for STA as a reward component, not just a diagnostic.\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 pilot (r5) showed prove_rate=0.15 at 100 steps with isr_pr_ratio=1.0 (ISR tracks prove_rate perfectly because the model hasn't yet learned structural shortcuts). At 500 steps, GRPO's policy gradient drives prove_rate up to ~0.28 — substantial improvement from the 0.12 baseline — but the model discovers extensional shortcuts: tautological premises, vacuous quantifiers, over-constrained formulas that prove trivially. These shortcuts yield solver-verified \\\"correct\\\" outputs with degraded internal structure, causing ISR to lag behind prove_rate. The isr_pr_ratio drops from ~0.92 (step 1-100, structure intact) to ~0.35 (step 400-500, structural degeneracy). This is the classic reward hacking signature: the model maximizes the proxy (execution reward) while degrading the latent quality dimension (structural fidelity). Three seeds provide sufficient signal to distinguish this trend from noise; std ~0.03 reflects Qwen3-4B's moderate sensitivity to initialization on FOLIO-scale data.\\n\"\n70\t  },\n71\t  {\n72\t    \"name\": \"FOL Exec+STA 500-Step Primary\",\n73\t    \"description\": \"FOL single-logic GRPO, execution+STA reward (full continuous STA), 500 steps, K=8, 3 seeds. Primary validation that STA reward prevents structural degradation while maintaining prove_rate.\\n\",\n74\t    \"results\": {\n75\t      \"prove_rate_mean\": 0.3,\n76\t      \"prove_rate_std\": 0.03,\n77\t      \"prove_rate_first100\": 0.16,\n78\t      \"prove_rate_last100\": 0.34,\n79\t      \"mean_isr_overall\": 0.19,\n80\t      \"mean_isr_last100\": 0.22,\n81\t      \"isr_pr_ratio_overall\": 0.63,\n82\t      \"isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.65,\n83\t      \"isr_pr_ratio_first100\": 0.48,\n84\t      \"parse_rate\": 1.0,\n85\t      \"combined_reward_mean\": 0.24,\n86\t      \"training_minutes\": 85,\n87\t      \"step_time_s\": 10.2\n88\t    },\n89\t    \"tables\": [\n90\t      {\n91\t        \"name\": \"Table 2b: Exec+STA Training Dynamics (3-seed mean ± std)\",\n92\t        \"columns\": [\n93\t          \"step_window\",\n94\t          \"prove_rate\",\n95\t          \"mean_isr\",\n96\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n97\t          \"combined_reward\"\n98\t        ],\n99\t        \"rows\": [\n100\t          [\n101\t            \"1-100\",\n102\t            \"0.16 ± 0.02\",\n103\t            \"0.08 ± 0.02\",\n104\t            \"0.48 ± 0.08\",\n105\t            \"0.13 ± 0.02\"\n106\t          ],\n107\t          [\n108\t            \"101-200\",\n109\t            \"0.22 ± 0.03\",\n110\t            \"0.13 ± 0.03\",\n111\t            \"0.58 ± 0.07\",\n112\t            \"0.17 ± 0.03\"\n113\t          ],\n114\t          [\n115\t            \"201-300\",\n116\t            \"0.26 ± 0.03\",\n117\t            \"0.17 ± 0.03\",\n118\t            \"0.64 ± 0.06\",\n119\t            \"0.21 ± 0.03\"\n120\t          ],\n121\t          [\n122\t            \"301-400\",\n123\t            \"0.28 ± 0.03\",\n124\t            \"0.19 ± 0.03\",\n125\t            \"0.67 ± 0.06\",\n126\t            \"0.23 ± 0.03\"\n127\t          ],\n128\t          [\n129\t            \"401-500\",\n130\t            \"0.34 ± 0.04\",\n131\t            \"0.22 ± 0.03\",\n132\t            \"0.65 ± 0.05\",\n133\t            \"0.28 ± 0.03\"\n134\t          ]\n135\t        ]\n136\t      }\n137\t    ],\n138\t    \"figures\": [],\n139\t    \"analysis\": \"Exec+STA achieves the best-tier prediction: prove_rate matches exec-only (0.30 vs 0.28, not statistically different) while maintaining isr_pr_ratio ≥0.5 throughout training (vs exec-only's degradation to 0.35). The slight prove_rate advantage (+0.02) emerges because STA penalizes degenerate shortcuts, forcing the model to discover structurally sound proving strategies that also happen to generalize better. The 7% wall-clock overhead (10.2s vs 9.5s per step) is negligible. This validates the core claim: STA reward is Pareto-improving (same accuracy, better structure) or at worst Pareto-neutral (same accuracy, maintained structure). The curriculum is load-bearing — without it (as in Phase 1 pilot), the 0.5 STA weight suppresses early prove_rate learning.\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 pilot (r5) showed exec+STA with 0.5/0.5 weighting depressed prove_rate (0.10 vs 0.15 for exec-only) because the STA penalty was too punitive at initialization, when the model produces mostly unprovable outputs. At 500 steps with curriculum weighting (sigmoid ramp from sta_weight=0.1→0.5 over first 200 steps), the model first learns to prove (prove_rate rises rapidly steps 1-200 under near-exec-only reward), then learns to prove structurally (ISR catches up steps 200-500 as STA weight activates). The key prediction: prove_rate matches or slightly exceeds exec-only (0.30 vs 0.28) because STA reward actively penalizes the degenerate shortcuts that exec-only relies on for its final prove_rate gains. The model is forced to find structurally sound proofs, which are more robust and transfer better. The isr_pr_ratio stabilizes at ~0.65 instead of degrading to 0.35 — this is the headline comparison. The initial dip in isr_pr_ratio (0.48 at step 1-100) reflects the low-STA-weight curriculum phase where behavior approximates exec-only; it then recovers as STA weight ramps up.\\n\"\n140\t  },\n141\t  {\n142\t    \"name\": \"FOL Held-Out Evaluation\",\n143\t    \"description\": \"Held-out test set evaluation comparing plan_001 (exec-only) vs plan_002 (exec+STA) checkpoints. Full STA metrics on unseen problems to measure generalization.\\n\",\n144\t    \"results\": {\n145\t      \"exec_only_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.24,\n146\t      \"exec_sta_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.26,\n147\t      \"exec_only_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.08,\n148\t      \"exec_sta_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.22,\n149\t      \"exec_only_held_out_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.33,\n150\t      \"exec_sta_held_out_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.85,\n151\t      \"exec_only_hacking_prevalence\": 0.45,\n152\t      \"exec_sta_hacking_prevalence\": 0.18\n153\t    },\n154\t    \"tables\": [\n155\t      {\n156\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Held-Out Generalization (best checkpoint per condition)\",\n157\t        \"columns\": [\n158\t          \"condition\",\n159\t          \"prove_rate\",\n160\t          \"mean_isr\",\n161\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n162\t          \"hacking_prevalence\"\n163\t        ],\n164\t        \"rows\": [\n165\t          [\n166\t            \"Base model (no training)\",\n167\t            \"0.12\",\n168\t            \"0.064\",\n169\t            \"0.53\",\n170\t            \"0.25\"\n171\t          ],\n172\t          [\n173\t            \"Exec-only (plan_001)\",\n174\t            \"0.24\",\n175\t            \"0.08\",\n176\t            \"0.33\",\n177\t            \"0.45\"\n178\t          ],\n179\t          [\n180\t            \"Exec+STA (plan_002)\",\n181\t            \"0.26\",\n182\t            \"0.22\",\n183\t            \"0.85\",\n184\t            \"0.18\"\n185\t          ],\n186\t          [\n187\t            \"Ground-truth reference\",\n188\t            \"0.18\",\n189\t            \"0.559\",\n190\t            \"—\",\n191\t            \"0.0\"\n192\t          ]\n193\t        ]\n194\t      }\n195\t    ],\n196\t    \"figures\": [],\n197\t    \"analysis\": \"The held-out results deliver the paper's strongest evidence. While prove_rate difference is modest and possibly not significant (0.24 vs 0.26, needs paired test), the ISR gap is dramatic (0.08 vs 0.22, ~2.75x). This means exec-only training produces models that \\\"prove\\\" held-out problems but via degenerate means (45% hacking rate), while exec+STA training produces models whose proofs are structurally meaningful. The isr_pr_ratio inversion (exec-only 0.33 < exec+STA 0.85) is the quantitative headline. Note the base model's isr_pr_ratio of 0.53 is higher than trained exec-only's 0.33 — training actively worsens structural quality under exec-only reward, a finding that underscores the need for STA.\\n\\n\\nHeld-out evaluation amplifies the structural gap because degenerate shortcuts overfit to training distribution. Exec-only model's prove_rate drops more on held-out (0.24 vs 0.28 in-distribution) because tautological/vacuous shortcuts are less likely to accidentally match held-out problem structures. Its held-out ISR drops further (0.08) because the learned shortcuts are even less structurally valid on new problems. Exec+STA model retains prove_rate better (0.26 vs 0.30, smaller drop) because structurally sound proofs generalize. Its ISR actually improves relative to isr_pr_ratio (0.85 vs 0.65 in-distribution) because the remaining proved outputs are genuinely well-structured — the degenerate easy wins that inflated training prove_rate without proportional ISR are gone. The hacking prevalence (fraction of proved outputs that are structurally degenerate by manual taxonomy) drops from 45% to 18%, directly supporting the \\\"STA corrects, not just detects\\\" narrative.\\n\"\n198\t  },\n199\t  {\n200\t    \"name\": \"STA O(1) vs O(N) Ablation at Scale\",\n201\t    \"description\": \"Rerun exec+STA with fast_isr_fol (O(1) tautology-only check) for 500 steps, 1 seed. Documents the negative result that coarse-grained ablation provides insufficient signal.\\n\",\n202\t    \"results\": {\n203\t      \"prove_rate\": 0.27,\n204\t      \"mean_isr_o1\": 0.04,\n205\t      \"isr_pr_ratio\": 0.15,\n206\t      \"isr_discriminability\": 0.08,\n207\t      \"combined_reward_correlation_with_on\": 0.12,\n208\t      \"training_trajectory\": \"indistinguishable from exec-only\"\n209\t    },\n210\t    \"tables\": [\n211\t      {\n212\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: O(1) vs O(N) Ablation Comparison\",\n213\t        \"columns\": [\n214\t          \"metric\",\n215\t          \"O(N) full STA\",\n216\t          \"O(1) fast_isr\",\n217\t          \"exec-only\"\n218\t        ],\n219\t        \"rows\": [\n220\t          [\n221\t            \"prove_rate (500 step)\",\n222\t            \"0.30\",\n223\t            \"0.27\",\n224\t            \"0.28\"\n225\t          ],\n226\t          [\n227\t            \"mean_isr\",\n228\t            \"0.19\",\n229\t            \"0.04\",\n230\t            \"0.11\"\n231\t          ],\n232\t          [\n233\t            \"isr_pr_ratio_last100\",\n234\t            \"0.65\",\n235\t            \"0.15\",\n236\t            \"0.35\"\n237\t          ],\n238\t          [\n239\t            \"isr_discriminability\",\n240\t            \"0.72\",\n241\t            \"0.08\",\n242\t            \"—\"\n243\t          ],\n244\t          [\n245\t            \"wall-clock overhead\",\n246\t            \"+7%\",\n247\t            \"+1%\",\n248\t            \"baseline\"\n249\t          ]\n250\t        ]\n251\t      }\n252\t    ],\n253\t    \"figures\": [],\n254\t    \"analysis\": \"The O(1) negative result is essential for the paper's methodology argument. It demonstrates that STA's value comes from comprehensive component ablation (O(N) cost per output), not from any single structural check. This motivates the 5-step RIP derivation protocol: each logic type has its own hacking modes, and the ablation set must cover them all. The O(1) result also preempts reviewer concern about STA's computational cost — yes, it's O(N) per component, but O(1) approximations demonstrably fail, so the cost is necessary. The slightly worse isr_pr_ratio for O(1) vs exec-only (0.15 vs 0.35) suggests the tautology penalty actively worsens structural diversity by forcing the model into a narrower set of non-tautological shortcuts.\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 r4 already showed O(1) failure at 100 steps. At 500 steps, the result is cleaner: fast_isr_fol checks only tautology (is the formula trivially true?), which catches only one of many hacking modes. The model quickly learns to avoid tautologies while discovering other shortcuts (vacuous quantifiers, over-constrained premises, redundant clauses) that O(1) cannot detect. Prove_rate (~0.27) and training dynamics are indistinguishable from exec-only because the O(1) ISR signal is near-zero for all non-tautological outputs (discriminability 0.08, meaning <8% of proved outputs receive any ISR signal). The combined reward reduces to effectively exec-only (correlation with full O(N) ISR is only 0.12). This is the principled negative result: domain-specific hacking modes require correspondingly granular ablation components. A single-check approximation captures one failure mode while the model routes around it to exploit others.\\n\"\n255\t  },\n256\t  {\n257\t    \"name\": \"STA Reward Weight Ablation\",\n258\t    \"description\": \"Test α/β ratios (0.3/0.7, 0.5/0.5, 0.7/0.3) × {fixed, sigmoid curriculum}. 6 runs × 500 steps × 1 seed = 6 runs total.\\n\",\n259\t    \"results\": {\n260\t      \"fixed_03_07_prove_rate\": 0.2,\n261\t      \"fixed_03_07_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.72,\n262\t      \"fixed_05_05_prove_rate\": 0.25,\n263\t      \"fixed_05_05_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.68,\n264\t      \"fixed_07_03_prove_rate\": 0.3,\n265\t      \"fixed_07_03_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.52,\n266\t      \"curriculum_03_07_prove_rate\": 0.26,\n267\t      \"curriculum_03_07_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.7,\n268\t      \"curriculum_05_05_prove_rate\": 0.3,\n269\t      \"curriculum_05_05_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.65,\n270\t      \"curriculum_07_03_prove_rate\": 0.32,\n271\t      \"curriculum_07_03_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.55,\n272\t      \"best_config\": \"curriculum α=0.5 β=0.5\"\n273\t    },\n274\t    \"tables\": [\n275\t      {\n276\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: Weight Ablation Results (500 steps, 1 seed)\",\n277\t        \"columns\": [\n278\t          \"α/β\",\n279\t          \"schedule\",\n280\t          \"prove_rate\",\n281\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n282\t          \"pr × ratio\"\n283\t        ],\n284\t        \"rows\": [\n285\t          [\n286\t            \"0.3/0.7\",\n287\t            \"fixed\",\n288\t            \"0.20\",\n289\t            \"0.72\",\n290\t            \"0.144\"\n291\t          ],\n292\t          [\n293\t            \"0.5/0.5\",\n294\t            \"fixed\",\n295\t            \"0.25\",\n296\t            \"0.68\",\n297\t            \"0.170\"\n298\t          ],\n299\t          [\n300\t            \"0.7/0.3\",\n301\t            \"fixed\",\n302\t            \"0.30\",\n303\t            \"0.52\",\n304\t            \"0.156\"\n305\t          ],\n306\t          [\n307\t            \"0.3/0.7\",\n308\t            \"curriculum\",\n309\t            \"0.26\",\n310\t            \"0.70\",\n311\t            \"0.182\"\n312\t          ],\n313\t          [\n314\t            \"0.5/0.5\",\n315\t            \"curriculum\",\n316\t            \"0.30\",\n317\t            \"0.65\",\n318\t            \"0.195\"\n319\t          ],\n320\t          [\n321\t            \"0.7/0.3\",\n322\t            \"curriculum\",\n323\t            \"0.32\",\n324\t            \"0.55\",\n325\t            \"0.176\"\n326\t          ],\n327\t          [\n328\t            \"1.0/0.0\",\n329\t            \"—\",\n330\t            \"0.28\",\n331\t            \"0.35\",\n332\t            \"0.098\"\n333\t          ]\n334\t        ]\n335\t      }\n336\t    ],\n337\t    \"figures\": [],\n338\t    \"analysis\": \"The weight ablation provides three key insights: (1) STA weight has a predictable dose-response relationship with structural quality, confirming it's a genuine training signal, not noise. (2) Sigmoid curriculum is a principled solution to the Phase 1 pilot's prove_rate depression problem — it recovers ~0.05 prove_rate compared to fixed weighting at matched terminal weights. (3) The Pareto analysis gives practitioners a concrete recommendation: use curriculum 0.5/0.5 for balanced accuracy-structure, or curriculum 0.7/0.3 if accuracy is the priority. This section also serves as an implicit response to \\\"how sensitive is STA to hyperparameters?\\\" — the answer is \\\"moderately, but curriculum scheduling makes it robust.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe weight ablation reveals a clear Pareto frontier trading prove_rate against structural quality. Fixed weighting shows the expected tradeoff: higher STA weight (β=0.7) produces highest isr_pr_ratio (0.72) but lowest prove_rate (0.20) because the STA penalty is too punitive during early training when the model cannot yet produce provable outputs. Lower STA weight (β=0.3) produces near-exec-only prove_rate (0.30) but only moderate structural benefit (isr_pr_ratio 0.52 vs exec-only's 0.35). The sigmoid curriculum consistently Pareto-dominates fixed weighting at the same terminal α/β: curriculum 0.5/0.5 matches fixed 0.7/0.3's prove_rate (0.30) while achieving higher isr_pr_ratio (0.65 vs 0.52). This is because the curriculum allows exec-reward-dominated early learning (the model first learns to prove) before STA activates (forcing structural quality). The optimal configuration is curriculum α=0.5/β=0.5 — it achieves the best prove_rate × isr_pr_ratio product (0.30 × 0.65 = 0.195) and lies at the Pareto knee. Curriculum α=0.7/β=0.3 achieves marginally higher prove_rate (0.32) but substantially lower structural quality — diminishing returns on the accuracy axis.\\n\"\n339\t  },\n340\t  {\n341\t    \"name\": \"Prolog Single-Logic GRPO\",\n342\t    \"description\": \"Prolog single-logic GRPO: exec-only vs exec+STA (500 steps, K=8, 1 seed). Second logic type to validate STA derivation protocol generality.\\n\",\n343\t    \"results\": {\n344\t      \"prolog_exec_only_prove_rate\": 0.22,\n345\t      \"prolog_exec_sta_prove_rate\": 0.24,\n346\t      \"prolog_exec_only_isr_mean\": 0.08,\n347\t      \"prolog_exec_sta_isr_mean\": 0.14,\n348\t      \"prolog_exec_only_isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.32,\n349\t      \"prolog_exec_sta_isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.58,\n350\t      \"prolog_isr_discriminability\": 0.45,\n351\t      \"prolog_hacking_modes\": [\n352\t        \"redundant_clauses\",\n353\t        \"catch_all_rules\",\n354\t        \"trivial_unification\"\n355\t      ]\n356\t    },\n357\t    \"tables\": [\n358\t      {\n359\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Prolog Single-Logic Results (500 steps, 1 seed)\",\n360\t        \"columns\": [\n361\t          \"condition\",\n362\t          \"prove_rate\",\n363\t          \"mean_isr\",\n364\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n365\t          \"discriminability\"\n366\t        ],\n367\t        \"rows\": [\n368\t          [\n369\t            \"Exec-only\",\n370\t            \"0.22\",\n371\t            \"0.08\",\n372\t            \"0.32\",\n373\t            \"—\"\n374\t          ],\n375\t          [\n376\t            \"Exec+STA\",\n377\t            \"0.24\",\n378\t            \"0.14\",\n379\t            \"0.58\",\n380\t            \"0.45\"\n381\t          ]\n382\t        ]\n383\t      }\n384\t    ],\n385\t    \"figures\": [],\n386\t    \"analysis\": \"Prolog results validate the STA derivation protocol's generality — the same 5-step procedure (pilot → shortcuts → classify → RIP map → design checks) produces a functional ISR signal for a structurally different logic type. The Prolog-specific hacking modes (redundant clauses, catch-all rules) are distinct from FOL's (tautological premises, vacuous quantifiers), confirming that hacking is logic-type-specific and STA catches it in both cases. Lower absolute metrics reflect the harder generation task, not methodology failure.\\n\\n\\nProlog STA instantiation uses rule ablation (remove each clause, check if query still succeeds) — the RIP mapping is: Reachability=clause is syntactically reachable from query, Infection=clause body binds variables used downstream, Propagation=removing clause changes query result. Prolog's hacking modes differ from FOL: instead of tautological premises, the model produces redundant clauses (rules that never fire), catch-all rules (match anything via anonymous variables), and trivial unification chains. These are detectable by STA because ablating redundant/catch-all clauses doesn't change the query result (ISR contribution = 0). Prove_rate is slightly lower than FOL (0.22 vs 0.28 for exec-only) because SWI-Prolog is more sensitive to syntactic errors and Qwen3-4B has less Prolog in its pretraining data. The exec+STA pattern replicates: ISR/PR ratio stabilizes (0.58 vs 0.32), confirming the methodology transfers. Discriminability (0.45) is lower than FOL (~0.72) because Prolog programs have fewer components per output (fewer clauses to ablate), creating a coarser ISR signal, but still sufficient for reward shaping.\\n\"\n387\t  },\n388\t  {\n389\t    \"name\": \"Multi-Logic GRPO (FOL+Prolog)\",\n390\t    \"description\": \"Multi-logic GRPO training on mixed FOL+Prolog data, exec-only vs exec+STA (500 steps, K=8, 1 seed). Tests whether joint training enables cross-logic transfer.\\n\",\n391\t    \"results\": {\n392\t      \"multi_fol_prove_rate\": 0.29,\n393\t      \"multi_prolog_prove_rate\": 0.25,\n394\t      \"multi_fol_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.62,\n395\t      \"multi_prolog_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.55,\n396\t      \"fol_improvement_over_single\": 0.01,\n397\t      \"prolog_improvement_over_single\": 0.03,\n398\t      \"cross_logic_transfer_signal\": \"positive\"\n399\t    },\n400\t    \"tables\": [\n401\t      {\n402\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Multi-Logic vs Single-Logic Comparison\",\n403\t        \"columns\": [\n404\t          \"logic\",\n405\t          \"training\",\n406\t          \"prove_rate\",\n407\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n408\t          \"Δ vs single\"\n409\t        ],\n410\t        \"rows\": [\n411\t          [\n412\t            \"FOL\",\n413\t            \"single exec+STA\",\n414\t            \"0.30\",\n415\t            \"0.65\",\n416\t            \"—\"\n417\t          ],\n418\t          [\n419\t            \"FOL\",\n420\t            \"multi exec+STA\",\n421\t            \"0.29\",\n422\t            \"0.62\",\n423\t            \"-0.01 / -0.03\"\n424\t          ],\n425\t          [\n426\t            \"Prolog\",\n427\t            \"single exec+STA\",\n428\t            \"0.24\",\n429\t            \"0.58\",\n430\t            \"—\"\n431\t          ],\n432\t          [\n433\t            \"Prolog\",\n434\t            \"multi exec+STA\",\n435\t            \"0.25\",\n436\t            \"0.55\",\n437\t            \"+0.01 / -0.03\"\n438\t          ]\n439\t        ]\n440\t      }\n441\t    ],\n442\t    \"figures\": [],\n443\t    \"analysis\": \"Multi-logic results are positive but not dramatic — the transfer signal is real but modest at 4B scale with 500 steps. This is expected: cross-logic transfer requires the model to abstract beyond surface syntax to shared logical reasoning patterns, which 4B models have limited capacity for. The paper frames this as \\\"measurably positive transfer\\\" rather than \\\"large effect,\\\" setting up future work on larger models. The slight isr_pr_ratio decrease under multi-logic training is an important nuance — mixed reward signals add noise, suggesting that logic-specific STA calibration (e.g., per-logic α/β) could improve multi-logic training.\\n\\n\\nMulti-logic training with mixed FOL+Prolog batches forces the model to maintain distinct formalization strategies per logic type, acting as implicit regularization against logic-specific hacking modes. FOL prove_rate is marginally better than single-logic (0.29 vs 0.28 exec-only baseline) because Prolog's different structural constraints provide auxiliary gradient signal. Prolog benefits more (+0.03 over single-logic's 0.24) because FOL training data is larger and the formal reasoning skills partially transfer (both involve quantifier scoping, logical connectives, variable binding). The isr_pr_ratio is slightly lower than single-logic exec+STA for both (0.62 vs 0.65 for FOL, 0.55 vs 0.58 for Prolog) because the mixed reward signal is noisier — each logic type's STA has different component counts and score distributions, creating gradient variance. The positive transfer signal is modest but measurable, supporting the Claim 2 narrative that GRPO over multiple formal languages exhibits useful cross-logic dynamics.\\n\"\n444\t  },\n445\t  {\n446\t    \"name\": \"Cross-Logic Transfer Evaluation\",\n447\t    \"description\": \"Zero-shot cross-logic evaluation: FOL-trained model on Prolog tasks, Prolog-trained model on FOL tasks. Measures transfer without explicit multi-logic training.\\n\",\n448\t    \"results\": {\n449\t      \"fol_model_on_prolog_prove_rate\": 0.16,\n450\t      \"prolog_model_on_fol_prove_rate\": 0.17,\n451\t      \"fol_model_on_prolog_delta_vs_base\": 0.04,\n452\t      \"prolog_model_on_fol_delta_vs_base\": 0.05,\n453\t      \"cross_logic_transfer_delta_mean\": 0.045\n454\t    },\n455\t    \"tables\": [\n456\t      {\n457\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: Cross-Logic Zero-Shot Transfer\",\n458\t        \"columns\": [\n459\t          \"source_model\",\n460\t          \"target_logic\",\n461\t          \"prove_rate\",\n462\t          \"Δ vs base\",\n463\t          \"mean_isr\"\n464\t        ],\n465\t        \"rows\": [\n466\t          [\n467\t            \"Base (no training)\",\n468\t            \"FOL\",\n469\t            \"0.12\",\n470\t            \"—\",\n471\t            \"0.064\"\n472\t          ],\n473\t          [\n474\t            \"Base (no training)\",\n475\t            \"Prolog\",\n476\t            \"0.12\",\n477\t            \"—\",\n478\t            \"0.05\"\n479\t          ],\n480\t          [\n481\t            \"FOL exec+STA\",\n482\t            \"Prolog\",\n483\t            \"0.16\",\n484\t            \"+0.04\",\n485\t            \"0.09\"\n486\t          ],\n487\t          [\n488\t            \"Prolog exec+STA\",\n489\t            \"FOL\",\n490\t            \"0.17\",\n491\t            \"+0.05\",\n492\t            \"0.10\"\n493\t          ],\n494\t          [\n495\t            \"FOL exec-only\",\n496\t            \"Prolog\",\n497\t            \"0.15\",\n498\t            \"+0.03\",\n499\t            \"0.06\"\n500\t          ],\n501\t          [\n502\t            \"Prolog exec-only\",\n503\t            \"FOL\",\n504\t            \"0.16\",\n505\t            \"+0.04\",\n506\t            \"0.07\"\n507\t          ]\n508\t        ]\n509\t      }\n510\t    ],\n511\t    \"figures\": [],\n512\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-logic transfer is positive but small, consistent with the \\\"best\\\" tier narrative that the methodology generalizes. The exec+STA advantage in transfer (+0.01 additional delta) is a subtle but important finding: structurally sound formalizations transfer better because they encode genuine logical structure rather than solver-specific shortcuts. This supports the paper's claim that STA doesn't just improve in-distribution metrics — it produces qualitatively different model capabilities.\\n\\n\\nCross-logic transfer delta [0.02, 0.08] predicted range. FOL→Prolog transfer (+0.04 prove_rate over base model's ~0.12 Prolog prove_rate) is modest because FOL training teaches quantifier handling and logical structure that partially maps to Prolog's clause semantics — both require correct variable scoping and logical decomposition. Prolog→FOL transfer (+0.05) is slightly larger because Prolog's rule-based reasoning develops a \\\"compositional formalization\\\" skill that transfers to FOL premise construction. Neither direction produces dramatic gains because the surface syntax is too different for shallow transfer, and 4B models at 500 steps don't develop deep enough logical abstractions for strong transfer. The positive delta (mean 0.045) is nonetheless noteworthy — it suggests shared latent formal reasoning capabilities that multi-logic training could amplify. Exec+STA-trained models show marginally higher transfer than exec-only trained models (+0.01 additional delta) because structurally sound formalizations encode more transferable logical patterns.\\n\"\n513\t  },\n514\t  {\n515\t    \"name\": \"SFT Baseline\",\n516\t    \"description\": \"Supervised finetuning on same FOLIO 500 data. Provides a non-RL baseline for GRPO comparison.\\n\",\n517\t    \"results\": {\n518\t      \"sft_prove_rate\": 0.2,\n519\t      \"sft_mean_isr\": 0.13,\n520\t      \"sft_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.65,\n521\t      \"sft_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.18,\n522\t      \"sft_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.12\n523\t    },\n524\t    \"tables\": [\n525\t      {\n526\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: SFT vs GRPO Comparison\",\n527\t        \"columns\": [\n528\t          \"method\",\n529\t          \"prove_rate\",\n530\t          \"mean_isr\",\n531\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n532\t          \"held_out_pr\"\n533\t        ],\n534\t        \"rows\": [\n535\t          [\n536\t            \"SFT\",\n537\t            \"0.20\",\n538\t            \"0.13\",\n539\t            \"0.65\",\n540\t            \"0.18\"\n541\t          ],\n542\t          [\n543\t            \"GRPO exec-only\",\n544\t            \"0.28\",\n545\t            \"0.11\",\n546\t            \"0.39\",\n547\t            \"0.24\"\n548\t          ],\n549\t          [\n550\t            \"GRPO exec+STA\",\n551\t            \"0.30\",\n552\t            \"0.19\",\n553\t            \"0.63\",\n554\t            \"0.26\"\n555\t          ],\n556\t          [\n557\t            \"Base model\",\n558\t            \"0.12\",\n559\t            \"0.064\",\n560\t            \"0.53\",\n561\t            \"0.12\"\n562\t          ]\n563\t        ]\n564\t      }\n565\t    ],\n566\t    \"figures\": [],\n567\t    \"analysis\": \"SFT baseline serves a narrative function: it shows that structural quality is a property of the training signal, not an inherent limitation of the model. SFT achieves good structure by imitating structured outputs; exec+STA achieves good structure by rewarding structural properties. The key advantage of exec+STA over SFT is accuracy (+0.10 prove_rate) — GRPO's exploration discovers proving strategies beyond the SFT training set.\\n\\n\\nSFT on ground-truth FOL translations achieves moderate prove_rate (0.20) — higher than base model (0.12) but lower than GRPO variants (0.28-0.30) — because SFT copies the distribution of training outputs without the exploration-exploitation dynamics that GRPO provides. Crucially, SFT maintains a healthy isr_pr_ratio (0.65) because it imitates human-written formalizations that are structurally sound by construction. This creates an interesting comparison: SFT has good structure but low accuracy; exec-only GRPO has high accuracy but degraded structure; exec+STA GRPO has both. The SFT baseline thus demonstrates that GRPO is necessary for accuracy and STA is necessary for maintaining the structural quality that SFT gets for free from human demonstrations. Held-out performance drops proportionally (0.18, 0.12) because SFT overfits to training problem templates more than GRPO does.\\n\"\n568\t  },\n569\t  {\n570\t    \"name\": \"SMT-LIB Single-Logic GRPO\",\n571\t    \"description\": \"SMT-LIB third logic type: exec-only vs exec+STA (500 steps, K=8, 1 seed). Stretch goal.\\n\",\n572\t    \"results\": {\n573\t      \"smt_exec_only_prove_rate\": 0.18,\n574\t      \"smt_exec_sta_prove_rate\": 0.19,\n575\t      \"smt_isr_pr_ratio_exec_only\": 0.38,\n576\t      \"smt_isr_pr_ratio_exec_sta\": 0.52,\n577\t      \"smt_isr_discriminability\": 0.35,\n578\t      \"smt_hacking_modes\": [\n579\t        \"trivial_assertions\",\n580\t        \"over_constrained_declares\",\n581\t        \"unused_variables\"\n582\t      ]\n583\t    },\n584\t    \"tables\": [\n585\t      {\n586\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: Three-Logic STA Summary\",\n587\t        \"columns\": [\n588\t          \"logic\",\n589\t          \"exec_only_pr\",\n590\t          \"exec_sta_pr\",\n591\t          \"exec_only_ratio\",\n592\t          \"exec_sta_ratio\",\n593\t          \"discriminability\"\n594\t        ],\n595\t        \"rows\": [\n596\t          [\n597\t            \"FOL (Prover9)\",\n598\t            \"0.28\",\n599\t            \"0.30\",\n600\t            \"0.35\",\n601\t            \"0.65\",\n602\t            \"0.72\"\n603\t          ],\n604\t          [\n605\t            \"Prolog (SWI)\",\n606\t            \"0.22\",\n607\t            \"0.24\",\n608\t            \"0.32\",\n609\t            \"0.58\",\n610\t            \"0.45\"\n611\t          ],\n612\t          [\n613\t            \"SMT-LIB (Z3)\",\n614\t            \"0.18\",\n615\t            \"0.19\",\n616\t            \"0.38\",\n617\t            \"0.52\",\n618\t            \"0.35\"\n619\t          ]\n620\t        ]\n621\t      }\n622\t    ],\n623\t    \"figures\": [],\n624\t    \"analysis\": \"SMT-LIB completes the three-logic validation, demonstrating the RIP derivation protocol produces functional STA instantiations across theorem proving (FOL), logic programming (Prolog), and constraint satisfaction (SMT-LIB). The diminishing effect magnitude correlates with ISR discriminability, which itself correlates with component independence — a finding that informs future STA instantiation design. Three logic types is sufficient for an EMNLP paper; the consistent qualitative pattern (exec+STA stabilizes structure) across structurally different formal languages is the core evidence for methodology generality.\\n\\n\\nSMT-LIB is the hardest logic type for Qwen3-4B: the syntax is S-expression-based (less represented in pretraining), Z3's error messages are less informative for learning, and the constraint satisfaction paradigm is structurally different from theorem proving. Prove_rate is lower (0.18 exec-only) but still above baseline. SMT-LIB STA uses constraint ablation: remove each assert/declare, check if (check-sat) result changes. Hacking modes are SMT-specific: trivial assertions (assert true), over-constrained declarations (declaring variables with unit domains), unused variables. ISR discriminability (0.35) is lower than FOL (0.72) because SMT-LIB programs have fewer independently ablatable components (many assertions are interdependent). The exec+STA benefit is present but smaller (isr_pr_ratio 0.52 vs 0.38) — still validates the methodology but with less dramatic effect, consistent with the coarser ISR signal.\\n\"\n625\t  },\n626\t  {\n627\t    \"name\": \"Qwen3-8B Scaling\",\n628\t    \"description\": \"Full MLSR pipeline on Qwen3-8B (1 seed, curriculum α=0.5/β=0.5, 500 steps). Model scale effect.\\n\",\n629\t    \"results\": {\n630\t      \"8b_exec_only_prove_rate\": 0.38,\n631\t      \"8b_exec_sta_prove_rate\": 0.4,\n632\t      \"8b_exec_only_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.4,\n633\t      \"8b_exec_sta_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.68,\n634\t      \"8b_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.34,\n635\t      \"8b_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.28,\n636\t      \"scaling_delta_prove_rate\": 0.1,\n637\t      \"scaling_delta_isr_ratio\": 0.03\n638\t    },\n639\t    \"tables\": [\n640\t      {\n641\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Scale Comparison (4B vs 8B, curriculum 0.5/0.5)\",\n642\t        \"columns\": [\n643\t          \"model\",\n644\t          \"condition\",\n645\t          \"prove_rate\",\n646\t          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n647\t          \"held_out_pr\"\n648\t        ],\n649\t        \"rows\": [\n650\t          [\n651\t            \"Qwen3-4B\",\n652\t            \"exec-only\",\n653\t            \"0.28\",\n654\t            \"0.35\",\n655\t            \"0.24\"\n656\t          ],\n657\t          [\n658\t            \"Qwen3-4B\",\n659\t            \"exec+STA\",\n660\t            \"0.30\",\n661\t            \"0.65\",\n662\t            \"0.26\"\n663\t          ],\n664\t          [\n665\t            \"Qwen3-8B\",\n666\t            \"exec-only\",\n667\t            \"0.38\",\n668\t            \"0.40\",\n669\t            \"0.32\"\n670\t          ],\n671\t          [\n672\t            \"Qwen3-8B\",\n673\t            \"exec+STA\",\n674\t            \"0.40\",\n675\t            \"0.68\",\n676\t            \"0.34\"\n677\t          ]\n678\t        ]\n679\t      }\n680\t    ],\n681\t    \"figures\": [],\n682\t    \"analysis\": \"8B results serve two functions: (1) demonstrate STA's scale-invariance — the methodology works at larger scales, not just small models where hacking is easier; (2) provide a more competitive absolute prove_rate (0.40) that looks better in a paper. The preserved isr_pr_ratio gap at 8B scale is the key finding — if the gap had closed, it would suggest structural degeneracy is a small-model artifact that STA addresses unnecessarily. Instead, the gap persists, confirming STA is needed regardless of model scale.\\n\\n\\nScaling from 4B to 8B increases prove_rate substantially (+0.10 for both conditions) because the larger model has better baseline FOL syntax knowledge and can explore a wider policy space during GRPO. The isr_pr_ratio pattern is preserved: exec-only degrades to 0.40 (slightly better than 4B's 0.35 — larger models hack slightly less aggressively because they have more capacity for genuine solutions alongside hacking shortcuts), while exec+STA maintains 0.68 (slightly above 4B's 0.65 — same structural reward prevents the same degradation mode). The STA benefit is approximately scale-invariant in relative terms (isr_pr_ratio improvement of ~0.28 at both scales), suggesting that structural degeneracy under exec-only reward is a fundamental property of GRPO optimization, not a small-model artifact. Held-out generalization improves proportionally.\\n\"\n683\t  },\n684\t  {\n685\t    \"name\": \"Hacking Characterization\",\n686\t    \"description\": \"Manual annotation of 200 outputs per condition (exec-only, exec+STA, base model, SFT) using extensional-shortcut taxonomy. No GPU required.\\n\",\n687\t    \"results\": {\n688\t      \"exec_only_hack_rate\": 0.45,\n689\t      \"exec_sta_hack_rate\": 0.18,\n690\t      \"base_model_hack_rate\": 0.25,\n691\t      \"sft_hack_rate\": 0.12,\n692\t      \"exec_only_taxonomy\": {\n693\t        \"tautological_premise\": 0.18,\n694\t        \"vacuous_quantifier\": 0.12,\n695\t        \"redundant_clause\": 0.08,\n696\t        \"over_constrained\": 0.05,\n697\t        \"other\": 0.02\n698\t      },\n699\t      \"exec_sta_taxonomy\": {\n700\t        \"tautological_premise\": 0.03,\n701\t        \"vacuous_quantifier\": 0.04,\n702\t        \"redundant_clause\": 0.06,\n703\t        \"over_constrained\": 0.03,\n704\t        \"other\": 0.02\n705\t      },\n706\t      \"inter_annotator_agreement_kappa\": 0.78\n707\t    },\n708\t    \"tables\": [\n709\t      {\n710\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Hacking Taxonomy Distribution (%)\",\n711\t        \"columns\": [\n712\t          \"shortcut_type\",\n713\t          \"base_model\",\n714\t          \"exec_only\",\n715\t          \"exec+STA\",\n716\t          \"SFT\"\n717\t        ],\n718\t        \"rows\": [\n719\t          [\n720\t            \"Tautological premise\",\n721\t            \"8\",\n722\t            \"18\",\n723\t            \"3\",\n724\t            \"2\"\n725\t          ],\n726\t          [\n727\t            \"Vacuous quantifier\",\n728\t            \"7\",\n729\t            \"12\",\n730\t            \"4\",\n731\t            \"3\"\n732\t          ],\n733\t          [\n734\t            \"Redundant clause\",\n735\t            \"5\",\n736\t            \"8\",\n737\t            \"6\",\n738\t            \"4\"\n739\t          ],\n740\t          [\n741\t            \"Over-constrained\",\n742\t            \"3\",\n743\t            \"5\",\n744\t            \"3\",\n745\t            \"2\"\n746\t          ],\n747\t          [\n748\t            \"Other/subtle\",\n749\t            \"2\",\n750\t            \"2\",\n751\t            \"2\",\n752\t            \"1\"\n753\t          ],\n754\t          [\n755\t            \"Total hack rate\",\n756\t            \"25\",\n757\t            \"45\",\n758\t            \"18\",\n759\t            \"12\"\n760\t          ]\n761\t        ]\n762\t      }\n763\t    ],\n764\t    \"figures\": [],\n765\t    \"analysis\": \"The hacking characterization provides the qualitative evidence that complements the quantitative ISR/PR ratio. Reviewers can see exactly what \\\"structural degeneracy\\\" means: 18% of exec-only proved outputs contain tautological premises like \\\"∀x(P(x) ∨ ¬P(x))\\\" that trivially satisfy the solver but contribute nothing to the formalization. STA's suppression of these modes (18%→3%) is directly attributable to ISR=0 scores for tautological components. The residual 18% hacking under exec+STA identifies limitations and future work: subtler shortcuts (redundant clauses with small ISR) require finer-grained ablation or curriculum-based emphasis on high-residual hacking modes.\\n\\nThe hacking taxonomy distinguishes five shortcut types, derived from the Phase 0 pilot analysis and RIP framework. Exec-only training amplifies hacking from 25% (base model) to 45% because GRPO's reward gradient selects for outputs that prove easily, and structural shortcuts prove more easily than genuine formalizations. The distribution is dominated by tautological premises (18%) and vacuous quantifiers (12%) — the two cheapest hacking modes (highest reward-per-character). Exec+STA training suppresses hacking to 18% (below base model's 25%) because STA penalizes these modes: tautological premises drop from 18%→3% (STA assigns ISR=0 to tautology components), vacuous quantifiers from 12%→4%. Residual hacking (18%) consists of subtler shortcuts that partial ISR doesn't fully penalize — primarily redundant clauses (6%) that have small but nonzero ISR contributions. SFT's low hack rate (12%) sets the floor — human-written outputs have few structural shortcuts. Inter-annotator agreement κ=0.78 (substantial) is achievable because the taxonomy categories have operational definitions tied to STA component ablation patterns.\\n\"\n766\t  }\n767\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"78583d99-c2f9-4acd-a60c-521218e1ab09","uuid":"f69b01d5-7220-4d0e-845a-42791bc47ab5","timestamp":"2026-05-13T22:14:11.870Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_3/projects/solver-trace-ablation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T215750/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"FOL Exec-Only 500-Step Baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"FOL single-logic GRPO, execution-only reward, 500 steps, K=8, 3 seeds. Establishes the structural degradation trajectory that STA is designed to prevent.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"prove_rate_mean\": 0.28,\n      \"prove_rate_std\": 0.03,\n      \"prove_rate_first100\": 0.18,\n      \"prove_rate_last100\": 0.33,\n      \"mean_isr_overall\": 0.11,\n      \"mean_isr_last100\": 0.09,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio_overall\": 0.39,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.35,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio_first100\": 0.92,\n      \"parse_rate\": 1.0,\n      \"training_minutes\": 79,\n      \"step_time_s\": 9.5\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2a: Exec-Only Training Dynamics (3-seed mean ± std)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step_window\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"mean_isr\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"reward_mean\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1-100\",\n            \"0.18 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.16 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.92 ± 0.05\",\n            \"0.18 ± 0.02\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"101-200\",\n            \"0.22 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.16 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.73 ± 0.08\",\n            \"0.22 ± 0.02\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"201-300\",\n            \"0.25 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.13 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.52 ± 0.07\",\n            \"0.25 ± 0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"301-400\",\n            \"0.27 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.11 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.41 ± 0.06\",\n            \"0.27 ± 0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"401-500\",\n            \"0.33 ± 0.04\",\n            \"0.09 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.35 ± 0.05\",\n            \"0.33 ± 0.04\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The exec-only trajectory confirms the core problem statement: execution-only GRPO optimizes prove_rate effectively (+133% from baseline) but introduces structural degeneracy that accelerates after step ~150. The declining isr_pr_ratio is the paper's primary diagnostic signal. Notably, prove_rate continues improving even as ISR declines, meaning standard evaluation (accuracy-only) would rate this as a successful training run. Only STA-derived ISR reveals the hidden failure mode. This motivates the need for STA as a reward component, not just a diagnostic.\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 pilot (r5) showed prove_rate=0.15 at 100 steps with isr_pr_ratio=1.0 (ISR tracks prove_rate perfectly because the model hasn't yet learned structural shortcuts). At 500 steps, GRPO's policy gradient drives prove_rate up to ~0.28 — substantial improvement from the 0.12 baseline — but the model discovers extensional shortcuts: tautological premises, vacuous quantifiers, over-constrained formulas that prove trivially. These shortcuts yield solver-verified \\\"correct\\\" outputs with degraded internal structure, causing ISR to lag behind prove_rate. The isr_pr_ratio drops from ~0.92 (step 1-100, structure intact) to ~0.35 (step 400-500, structural degeneracy). This is the classic reward hacking signature: the model maximizes the proxy (execution reward) while degrading the latent quality dimension (structural fidelity). Three seeds provide sufficient signal to distinguish this trend from noise; std ~0.03 reflects Qwen3-4B's moderate sensitivity to initialization on FOLIO-scale data.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"FOL Exec+STA 500-Step Primary\",\n    \"description\": \"FOL single-logic GRPO, execution+STA reward (full continuous STA), 500 steps, K=8, 3 seeds. Primary validation that STA reward prevents structural degradation while maintaining prove_rate.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"prove_rate_mean\": 0.3,\n      \"prove_rate_std\": 0.03,\n      \"prove_rate_first100\": 0.16,\n      \"prove_rate_last100\": 0.34,\n      \"mean_isr_overall\": 0.19,\n      \"mean_isr_last100\": 0.22,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio_overall\": 0.63,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.65,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio_first100\": 0.48,\n      \"parse_rate\": 1.0,\n      \"combined_reward_mean\": 0.24,\n      \"training_minutes\": 85,\n      \"step_time_s\": 10.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2b: Exec+STA Training Dynamics (3-seed mean ± std)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step_window\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"mean_isr\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"combined_reward\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1-100\",\n            \"0.16 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.08 ± 0.02\",\n            \"0.48 ± 0.08\",\n            \"0.13 ± 0.02\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"101-200\",\n            \"0.22 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.13 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.58 ± 0.07\",\n            \"0.17 ± 0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"201-300\",\n            \"0.26 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.17 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.64 ± 0.06\",\n            \"0.21 ± 0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"301-400\",\n            \"0.28 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.19 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.67 ± 0.06\",\n            \"0.23 ± 0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"401-500\",\n            \"0.34 ± 0.04\",\n            \"0.22 ± 0.03\",\n            \"0.65 ± 0.05\",\n            \"0.28 ± 0.03\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Exec+STA achieves the best-tier prediction: prove_rate matches exec-only (0.30 vs 0.28, not statistically different) while maintaining isr_pr_ratio ≥0.5 throughout training (vs exec-only's degradation to 0.35). The slight prove_rate advantage (+0.02) emerges because STA penalizes degenerate shortcuts, forcing the model to discover structurally sound proving strategies that also happen to generalize better. The 7% wall-clock overhead (10.2s vs 9.5s per step) is negligible. This validates the core claim: STA reward is Pareto-improving (same accuracy, better structure) or at worst Pareto-neutral (same accuracy, maintained structure). The curriculum is load-bearing — without it (as in Phase 1 pilot), the 0.5 STA weight suppresses early prove_rate learning.\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 pilot (r5) showed exec+STA with 0.5/0.5 weighting depressed prove_rate (0.10 vs 0.15 for exec-only) because the STA penalty was too punitive at initialization, when the model produces mostly unprovable outputs. At 500 steps with curriculum weighting (sigmoid ramp from sta_weight=0.1→0.5 over first 200 steps), the model first learns to prove (prove_rate rises rapidly steps 1-200 under near-exec-only reward), then learns to prove structurally (ISR catches up steps 200-500 as STA weight activates). The key prediction: prove_rate matches or slightly exceeds exec-only (0.30 vs 0.28) because STA reward actively penalizes the degenerate shortcuts that exec-only relies on for its final prove_rate gains. The model is forced to find structurally sound proofs, which are more robust and transfer better. The isr_pr_ratio stabilizes at ~0.65 instead of degrading to 0.35 — this is the headline comparison. The initial dip in isr_pr_ratio (0.48 at step 1-100) reflects the low-STA-weight curriculum phase where behavior approximates exec-only; it then recovers as STA weight ramps up.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"FOL Held-Out Evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Held-out test set evaluation comparing plan_001 (exec-only) vs plan_002 (exec+STA) checkpoints. Full STA metrics on unseen problems to measure generalization.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"exec_only_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.24,\n      \"exec_sta_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.26,\n      \"exec_only_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.08,\n      \"exec_sta_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.22,\n      \"exec_only_held_out_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.33,\n      \"exec_sta_held_out_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.85,\n      \"exec_only_hacking_prevalence\": 0.45,\n      \"exec_sta_hacking_prevalence\": 0.18\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Held-Out Generalization (best checkpoint per condition)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"condition\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"mean_isr\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"hacking_prevalence\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Base model (no training)\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.064\",\n            \"0.53\",\n            \"0.25\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Exec-only (plan_001)\",\n            \"0.24\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.33\",\n            \"0.45\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Exec+STA (plan_002)\",\n            \"0.26\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"0.85\",\n            \"0.18\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ground-truth reference\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"0.559\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.0\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The held-out results deliver the paper's strongest evidence. While prove_rate difference is modest and possibly not significant (0.24 vs 0.26, needs paired test), the ISR gap is dramatic (0.08 vs 0.22, ~2.75x). This means exec-only training produces models that \\\"prove\\\" held-out problems but via degenerate means (45% hacking rate), while exec+STA training produces models whose proofs are structurally meaningful. The isr_pr_ratio inversion (exec-only 0.33 < exec+STA 0.85) is the quantitative headline. Note the base model's isr_pr_ratio of 0.53 is higher than trained exec-only's 0.33 — training actively worsens structural quality under exec-only reward, a finding that underscores the need for STA.\\n\\n\\nHeld-out evaluation amplifies the structural gap because degenerate shortcuts overfit to training distribution. Exec-only model's prove_rate drops more on held-out (0.24 vs 0.28 in-distribution) because tautological/vacuous shortcuts are less likely to accidentally match held-out problem structures. Its held-out ISR drops further (0.08) because the learned shortcuts are even less structurally valid on new problems. Exec+STA model retains prove_rate better (0.26 vs 0.30, smaller drop) because structurally sound proofs generalize. Its ISR actually improves relative to isr_pr_ratio (0.85 vs 0.65 in-distribution) because the remaining proved outputs are genuinely well-structured — the degenerate easy wins that inflated training prove_rate without proportional ISR are gone. The hacking prevalence (fraction of proved outputs that are structurally degenerate by manual taxonomy) drops from 45% to 18%, directly supporting the \\\"STA corrects, not just detects\\\" narrative.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"STA O(1) vs O(N) Ablation at Scale\",\n    \"description\": \"Rerun exec+STA with fast_isr_fol (O(1) tautology-only check) for 500 steps, 1 seed. Documents the negative result that coarse-grained ablation provides insufficient signal.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"prove_rate\": 0.27,\n      \"mean_isr_o1\": 0.04,\n      \"isr_pr_ratio\": 0.15,\n      \"isr_discriminability\": 0.08,\n      \"combined_reward_correlation_with_on\": 0.12,\n      \"training_trajectory\": \"indistinguishable from exec-only\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: O(1) vs O(N) Ablation Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"O(N) full STA\",\n          \"O(1) fast_isr\",\n          \"exec-only\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"prove_rate (500 step)\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.27\",\n            \"0.28\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mean_isr\",\n            \"0.19\",\n            \"0.04\",\n            \"0.11\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"isr_pr_ratio_last100\",\n            \"0.65\",\n            \"0.15\",\n            \"0.35\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"isr_discriminability\",\n            \"0.72\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"wall-clock overhead\",\n            \"+7%\",\n            \"+1%\",\n            \"baseline\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The O(1) negative result is essential for the paper's methodology argument. It demonstrates that STA's value comes from comprehensive component ablation (O(N) cost per output), not from any single structural check. This motivates the 5-step RIP derivation protocol: each logic type has its own hacking modes, and the ablation set must cover them all. The O(1) result also preempts reviewer concern about STA's computational cost — yes, it's O(N) per component, but O(1) approximations demonstrably fail, so the cost is necessary. The slightly worse isr_pr_ratio for O(1) vs exec-only (0.15 vs 0.35) suggests the tautology penalty actively worsens structural diversity by forcing the model into a narrower set of non-tautological shortcuts.\\n\\n\\nPhase 1 r4 already showed O(1) failure at 100 steps. At 500 steps, the result is cleaner: fast_isr_fol checks only tautology (is the formula trivially true?), which catches only one of many hacking modes. The model quickly learns to avoid tautologies while discovering other shortcuts (vacuous quantifiers, over-constrained premises, redundant clauses) that O(1) cannot detect. Prove_rate (~0.27) and training dynamics are indistinguishable from exec-only because the O(1) ISR signal is near-zero for all non-tautological outputs (discriminability 0.08, meaning <8% of proved outputs receive any ISR signal). The combined reward reduces to effectively exec-only (correlation with full O(N) ISR is only 0.12). This is the principled negative result: domain-specific hacking modes require correspondingly granular ablation components. A single-check approximation captures one failure mode while the model routes around it to exploit others.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"STA Reward Weight Ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Test α/β ratios (0.3/0.7, 0.5/0.5, 0.7/0.3) × {fixed, sigmoid curriculum}. 6 runs × 500 steps × 1 seed = 6 runs total.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"fixed_03_07_prove_rate\": 0.2,\n      \"fixed_03_07_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.72,\n      \"fixed_05_05_prove_rate\": 0.25,\n      \"fixed_05_05_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.68,\n      \"fixed_07_03_prove_rate\": 0.3,\n      \"fixed_07_03_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.52,\n      \"curriculum_03_07_prove_rate\": 0.26,\n      \"curriculum_03_07_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.7,\n      \"curriculum_05_05_prove_rate\": 0.3,\n      \"curriculum_05_05_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.65,\n      \"curriculum_07_03_prove_rate\": 0.32,\n      \"curriculum_07_03_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.55,\n      \"best_config\": \"curriculum α=0.5 β=0.5\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: Weight Ablation Results (500 steps, 1 seed)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"α/β\",\n          \"schedule\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"pr × ratio\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0.3/0.7\",\n            \"fixed\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"0.72\",\n            \"0.144\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.5/0.5\",\n            \"fixed\",\n            \"0.25\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"0.170\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.7/0.3\",\n            \"fixed\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.52\",\n            \"0.156\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.3/0.7\",\n            \"curriculum\",\n            \"0.26\",\n            \"0.70\",\n            \"0.182\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.5/0.5\",\n            \"curriculum\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.65\",\n            \"0.195\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.7/0.3\",\n            \"curriculum\",\n            \"0.32\",\n            \"0.55\",\n            \"0.176\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.0/0.0\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.098\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The weight ablation provides three key insights: (1) STA weight has a predictable dose-response relationship with structural quality, confirming it's a genuine training signal, not noise. (2) Sigmoid curriculum is a principled solution to the Phase 1 pilot's prove_rate depression problem — it recovers ~0.05 prove_rate compared to fixed weighting at matched terminal weights. (3) The Pareto analysis gives practitioners a concrete recommendation: use curriculum 0.5/0.5 for balanced accuracy-structure, or curriculum 0.7/0.3 if accuracy is the priority. This section also serves as an implicit response to \\\"how sensitive is STA to hyperparameters?\\\" — the answer is \\\"moderately, but curriculum scheduling makes it robust.\\\"\\n\\n\\nThe weight ablation reveals a clear Pareto frontier trading prove_rate against structural quality. Fixed weighting shows the expected tradeoff: higher STA weight (β=0.7) produces highest isr_pr_ratio (0.72) but lowest prove_rate (0.20) because the STA penalty is too punitive during early training when the model cannot yet produce provable outputs. Lower STA weight (β=0.3) produces near-exec-only prove_rate (0.30) but only moderate structural benefit (isr_pr_ratio 0.52 vs exec-only's 0.35). The sigmoid curriculum consistently Pareto-dominates fixed weighting at the same terminal α/β: curriculum 0.5/0.5 matches fixed 0.7/0.3's prove_rate (0.30) while achieving higher isr_pr_ratio (0.65 vs 0.52). This is because the curriculum allows exec-reward-dominated early learning (the model first learns to prove) before STA activates (forcing structural quality). The optimal configuration is curriculum α=0.5/β=0.5 — it achieves the best prove_rate × isr_pr_ratio product (0.30 × 0.65 = 0.195) and lies at the Pareto knee. Curriculum α=0.7/β=0.3 achieves marginally higher prove_rate (0.32) but substantially lower structural quality — diminishing returns on the accuracy axis.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Prolog Single-Logic GRPO\",\n    \"description\": \"Prolog single-logic GRPO: exec-only vs exec+STA (500 steps, K=8, 1 seed). Second logic type to validate STA derivation protocol generality.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"prolog_exec_only_prove_rate\": 0.22,\n      \"prolog_exec_sta_prove_rate\": 0.24,\n      \"prolog_exec_only_isr_mean\": 0.08,\n      \"prolog_exec_sta_isr_mean\": 0.14,\n      \"prolog_exec_only_isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.32,\n      \"prolog_exec_sta_isr_pr_ratio_last100\": 0.58,\n      \"prolog_isr_discriminability\": 0.45,\n      \"prolog_hacking_modes\": [\n        \"redundant_clauses\",\n        \"catch_all_rules\",\n        \"trivial_unification\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Prolog Single-Logic Results (500 steps, 1 seed)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"condition\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"mean_isr\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"discriminability\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Exec-only\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"0.08\",\n            \"0.32\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Exec+STA\",\n            \"0.24\",\n            \"0.14\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.45\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Prolog results validate the STA derivation protocol's generality — the same 5-step procedure (pilot → shortcuts → classify → RIP map → design checks) produces a functional ISR signal for a structurally different logic type. The Prolog-specific hacking modes (redundant clauses, catch-all rules) are distinct from FOL's (tautological premises, vacuous quantifiers), confirming that hacking is logic-type-specific and STA catches it in both cases. Lower absolute metrics reflect the harder generation task, not methodology failure.\\n\\n\\nProlog STA instantiation uses rule ablation (remove each clause, check if query still succeeds) — the RIP mapping is: Reachability=clause is syntactically reachable from query, Infection=clause body binds variables used downstream, Propagation=removing clause changes query result. Prolog's hacking modes differ from FOL: instead of tautological premises, the model produces redundant clauses (rules that never fire), catch-all rules (match anything via anonymous variables), and trivial unification chains. These are detectable by STA because ablating redundant/catch-all clauses doesn't change the query result (ISR contribution = 0). Prove_rate is slightly lower than FOL (0.22 vs 0.28 for exec-only) because SWI-Prolog is more sensitive to syntactic errors and Qwen3-4B has less Prolog in its pretraining data. The exec+STA pattern replicates: ISR/PR ratio stabilizes (0.58 vs 0.32), confirming the methodology transfers. Discriminability (0.45) is lower than FOL (~0.72) because Prolog programs have fewer components per output (fewer clauses to ablate), creating a coarser ISR signal, but still sufficient for reward shaping.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Multi-Logic GRPO (FOL+Prolog)\",\n    \"description\": \"Multi-logic GRPO training on mixed FOL+Prolog data, exec-only vs exec+STA (500 steps, K=8, 1 seed). Tests whether joint training enables cross-logic transfer.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"multi_fol_prove_rate\": 0.29,\n      \"multi_prolog_prove_rate\": 0.25,\n      \"multi_fol_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.62,\n      \"multi_prolog_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.55,\n      \"fol_improvement_over_single\": 0.01,\n      \"prolog_improvement_over_single\": 0.03,\n      \"cross_logic_transfer_signal\": \"positive\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Multi-Logic vs Single-Logic Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"logic\",\n          \"training\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"Δ vs single\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"FOL\",\n            \"single exec+STA\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.65\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FOL\",\n            \"multi exec+STA\",\n            \"0.29\",\n            \"0.62\",\n            \"-0.01 / -0.03\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Prolog\",\n            \"single exec+STA\",\n            \"0.24\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Prolog\",\n            \"multi exec+STA\",\n            \"0.25\",\n            \"0.55\",\n            \"+0.01 / -0.03\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Multi-logic results are positive but not dramatic — the transfer signal is real but modest at 4B scale with 500 steps. This is expected: cross-logic transfer requires the model to abstract beyond surface syntax to shared logical reasoning patterns, which 4B models have limited capacity for. The paper frames this as \\\"measurably positive transfer\\\" rather than \\\"large effect,\\\" setting up future work on larger models. The slight isr_pr_ratio decrease under multi-logic training is an important nuance — mixed reward signals add noise, suggesting that logic-specific STA calibration (e.g., per-logic α/β) could improve multi-logic training.\\n\\n\\nMulti-logic training with mixed FOL+Prolog batches forces the model to maintain distinct formalization strategies per logic type, acting as implicit regularization against logic-specific hacking modes. FOL prove_rate is marginally better than single-logic (0.29 vs 0.28 exec-only baseline) because Prolog's different structural constraints provide auxiliary gradient signal. Prolog benefits more (+0.03 over single-logic's 0.24) because FOL training data is larger and the formal reasoning skills partially transfer (both involve quantifier scoping, logical connectives, variable binding). The isr_pr_ratio is slightly lower than single-logic exec+STA for both (0.62 vs 0.65 for FOL, 0.55 vs 0.58 for Prolog) because the mixed reward signal is noisier — each logic type's STA has different component counts and score distributions, creating gradient variance. The positive transfer signal is modest but measurable, supporting the Claim 2 narrative that GRPO over multiple formal languages exhibits useful cross-logic dynamics.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-Logic Transfer Evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Zero-shot cross-logic evaluation: FOL-trained model on Prolog tasks, Prolog-trained model on FOL tasks. Measures transfer without explicit multi-logic training.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"fol_model_on_prolog_prove_rate\": 0.16,\n      \"prolog_model_on_fol_prove_rate\": 0.17,\n      \"fol_model_on_prolog_delta_vs_base\": 0.04,\n      \"prolog_model_on_fol_delta_vs_base\": 0.05,\n      \"cross_logic_transfer_delta_mean\": 0.045\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: Cross-Logic Zero-Shot Transfer\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"source_model\",\n          \"target_logic\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"Δ vs base\",\n          \"mean_isr\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Base (no training)\",\n            \"FOL\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.064\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Base (no training)\",\n            \"Prolog\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.05\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FOL exec+STA\",\n            \"Prolog\",\n            \"0.16\",\n            \"+0.04\",\n            \"0.09\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Prolog exec+STA\",\n            \"FOL\",\n            \"0.17\",\n            \"+0.05\",\n            \"0.10\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"FOL exec-only\",\n            \"Prolog\",\n            \"0.15\",\n            \"+0.03\",\n            \"0.06\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Prolog exec-only\",\n            \"FOL\",\n            \"0.16\",\n            \"+0.04\",\n            \"0.07\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-logic transfer is positive but small, consistent with the \\\"best\\\" tier narrative that the methodology generalizes. The exec+STA advantage in transfer (+0.01 additional delta) is a subtle but important finding: structurally sound formalizations transfer better because they encode genuine logical structure rather than solver-specific shortcuts. This supports the paper's claim that STA doesn't just improve in-distribution metrics — it produces qualitatively different model capabilities.\\n\\n\\nCross-logic transfer delta [0.02, 0.08] predicted range. FOL→Prolog transfer (+0.04 prove_rate over base model's ~0.12 Prolog prove_rate) is modest because FOL training teaches quantifier handling and logical structure that partially maps to Prolog's clause semantics — both require correct variable scoping and logical decomposition. Prolog→FOL transfer (+0.05) is slightly larger because Prolog's rule-based reasoning develops a \\\"compositional formalization\\\" skill that transfers to FOL premise construction. Neither direction produces dramatic gains because the surface syntax is too different for shallow transfer, and 4B models at 500 steps don't develop deep enough logical abstractions for strong transfer. The positive delta (mean 0.045) is nonetheless noteworthy — it suggests shared latent formal reasoning capabilities that multi-logic training could amplify. Exec+STA-trained models show marginally higher transfer than exec-only trained models (+0.01 additional delta) because structurally sound formalizations encode more transferable logical patterns.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SFT Baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Supervised finetuning on same FOLIO 500 data. Provides a non-RL baseline for GRPO comparison.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"sft_prove_rate\": 0.2,\n      \"sft_mean_isr\": 0.13,\n      \"sft_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.65,\n      \"sft_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.18,\n      \"sft_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.12\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: SFT vs GRPO Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"mean_isr\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"held_out_pr\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SFT\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"0.13\",\n            \"0.65\",\n            \"0.18\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRPO exec-only\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"0.39\",\n            \"0.24\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRPO exec+STA\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.19\",\n            \"0.63\",\n            \"0.26\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Base model\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.064\",\n            \"0.53\",\n            \"0.12\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SFT baseline serves a narrative function: it shows that structural quality is a property of the training signal, not an inherent limitation of the model. SFT achieves good structure by imitating structured outputs; exec+STA achieves good structure by rewarding structural properties. The key advantage of exec+STA over SFT is accuracy (+0.10 prove_rate) — GRPO's exploration discovers proving strategies beyond the SFT training set.\\n\\n\\nSFT on ground-truth FOL translations achieves moderate prove_rate (0.20) — higher than base model (0.12) but lower than GRPO variants (0.28-0.30) — because SFT copies the distribution of training outputs without the exploration-exploitation dynamics that GRPO provides. Crucially, SFT maintains a healthy isr_pr_ratio (0.65) because it imitates human-written formalizations that are structurally sound by construction. This creates an interesting comparison: SFT has good structure but low accuracy; exec-only GRPO has high accuracy but degraded structure; exec+STA GRPO has both. The SFT baseline thus demonstrates that GRPO is necessary for accuracy and STA is necessary for maintaining the structural quality that SFT gets for free from human demonstrations. Held-out performance drops proportionally (0.18, 0.12) because SFT overfits to training problem templates more than GRPO does.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"SMT-LIB Single-Logic GRPO\",\n    \"description\": \"SMT-LIB third logic type: exec-only vs exec+STA (500 steps, K=8, 1 seed). Stretch goal.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"smt_exec_only_prove_rate\": 0.18,\n      \"smt_exec_sta_prove_rate\": 0.19,\n      \"smt_isr_pr_ratio_exec_only\": 0.38,\n      \"smt_isr_pr_ratio_exec_sta\": 0.52,\n      \"smt_isr_discriminability\": 0.35,\n      \"smt_hacking_modes\": [\n        \"trivial_assertions\",\n        \"over_constrained_declares\",\n        \"unused_variables\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: Three-Logic STA Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"logic\",\n          \"exec_only_pr\",\n          \"exec_sta_pr\",\n          \"exec_only_ratio\",\n          \"exec_sta_ratio\",\n          \"discriminability\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"FOL (Prover9)\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.65\",\n            \"0.72\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Prolog (SWI)\",\n            \"0.22\",\n            \"0.24\",\n            \"0.32\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.45\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SMT-LIB (Z3)\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"0.19\",\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"0.52\",\n            \"0.35\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"SMT-LIB completes the three-logic validation, demonstrating the RIP derivation protocol produces functional STA instantiations across theorem proving (FOL), logic programming (Prolog), and constraint satisfaction (SMT-LIB). The diminishing effect magnitude correlates with ISR discriminability, which itself correlates with component independence — a finding that informs future STA instantiation design. Three logic types is sufficient for an EMNLP paper; the consistent qualitative pattern (exec+STA stabilizes structure) across structurally different formal languages is the core evidence for methodology generality.\\n\\n\\nSMT-LIB is the hardest logic type for Qwen3-4B: the syntax is S-expression-based (less represented in pretraining), Z3's error messages are less informative for learning, and the constraint satisfaction paradigm is structurally different from theorem proving. Prove_rate is lower (0.18 exec-only) but still above baseline. SMT-LIB STA uses constraint ablation: remove each assert/declare, check if (check-sat) result changes. Hacking modes are SMT-specific: trivial assertions (assert true), over-constrained declarations (declaring variables with unit domains), unused variables. ISR discriminability (0.35) is lower than FOL (0.72) because SMT-LIB programs have fewer independently ablatable components (many assertions are interdependent). The exec+STA benefit is present but smaller (isr_pr_ratio 0.52 vs 0.38) — still validates the methodology but with less dramatic effect, consistent with the coarser ISR signal.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Qwen3-8B Scaling\",\n    \"description\": \"Full MLSR pipeline on Qwen3-8B (1 seed, curriculum α=0.5/β=0.5, 500 steps). Model scale effect.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"8b_exec_only_prove_rate\": 0.38,\n      \"8b_exec_sta_prove_rate\": 0.4,\n      \"8b_exec_only_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.4,\n      \"8b_exec_sta_isr_pr_ratio\": 0.68,\n      \"8b_held_out_prove_rate\": 0.34,\n      \"8b_held_out_isr_mean\": 0.28,\n      \"scaling_delta_prove_rate\": 0.1,\n      \"scaling_delta_isr_ratio\": 0.03\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Scale Comparison (4B vs 8B, curriculum 0.5/0.5)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model\",\n          \"condition\",\n          \"prove_rate\",\n          \"isr_pr_ratio\",\n          \"held_out_pr\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-4B\",\n            \"exec-only\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.24\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-4B\",\n            \"exec+STA\",\n            \"0.30\",\n            \"0.65\",\n            \"0.26\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-8B\",\n            \"exec-only\",\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"0.40\",\n            \"0.32\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen3-8B\",\n            \"exec+STA\",\n            \"0.40\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"0.34\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"8B results serve two functions: (1) demonstrate STA's scale-invariance — the methodology works at larger scales, not just small models where hacking is easier; (2) provide a more competitive absolute prove_rate (0.40) that looks better in a paper. The preserved isr_pr_ratio gap at 8B scale is the key finding — if the gap had closed, it would suggest structural degeneracy is a small-model artifact that STA addresses unnecessarily. Instead, the gap persists, confirming STA is needed regardless of model scale.\\n\\n\\nScaling from 4B to 8B increases prove_rate substantially (+0.10 for both conditions) because the larger model has better baseline FOL syntax knowledge and can explore a wider policy space during GRPO. The isr_pr_ratio pattern is preserved: exec-only degrades to 0.40 (slightly better than 4B's 0.35 — larger models hack slightly less aggressively because they have more capacity for genuine solutions alongside hacking shortcuts), while exec+STA maintains 0.68 (slightly above 4B's 0.65 — same structural reward prevents the same degradation mode). The STA benefit is approximately scale-invariant in relative terms (isr_pr_ratio improvement of ~0.28 at both scales), suggesting that structural degeneracy under exec-only reward is a fundamental property of GRPO optimization, not a small-model artifact. Held-out generalization improves proportionally.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Hacking Characterization\",\n    \"description\": \"Manual annotation of 200 outputs per condition (exec-only, exec+STA, base model, SFT) using extensional-shortcut taxonomy. No GPU required.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"exec_only_hack_rate\": 0.45,\n      \"exec_sta_hack_rate\": 0.18,\n      \"base_model_hack_rate\": 0.25,\n      \"sft_hack_rate\": 0.12,\n      \"exec_only_taxonomy\": {\n        \"tautological_premise\": 0.18,\n        \"vacuous_quantifier\": 0.12,\n        \"redundant_clause\": 0.08,\n        \"over_constrained\": 0.05,\n        \"other\": 0.02\n      },\n      \"exec_sta_taxonomy\": {\n        \"tautological_premise\": 0.03,\n        \"vacuous_quantifier\": 0.04,\n        \"redundant_clause\": 0.06,\n        \"over_constrained\": 0.03,\n        \"other\": 0.02\n      },\n      \"inter_annotator_agreement_kappa\": 0.78\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Hacking Taxonomy Distribution (%)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"shortcut_type\",\n          \"base_model\",\n          \"exec_only\",\n          \"exec+STA\",\n          \"SFT\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Tautological premise\",\n            \"8\",\n            \"18\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Vacuous quantifier\",\n            \"7\",\n            \"12\",\n            \"4\",\n            \"3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Redundant clause\",\n            \"5\",\n            \"8\",\n            \"6\",\n            \"4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Over-constrained\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"5\",\n            \"3\",\n            \"2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Other/subtle\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"2\",\n            \"1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Total hack rate\",\n            \"25\",\n            \"45\",\n            \"18\",\n            \"12\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The hacking characterization provides the qualitative evidence that complements the quantitative ISR/PR ratio. Reviewers can see exactly what \\\"structural degeneracy\\\" means: 18% of exec-only proved outputs contain tautological premises like \\\"∀x(P(x) ∨ ¬P(x))\\\" that trivially satisfy the solver but contribute nothing to the formalization. STA's suppression of these modes (18%→3%) is directly attributable to ISR=0 scores for tautological components. The residual 18% hacking under exec+STA identifies limitations and future work: subtler shortcuts (redundant clauses with small ISR) require finer-grained ablation or curriculum-based emphasis on high-residual hacking modes.\\n\\nThe hacking taxonomy distinguishes five shortcut types, derived from the Phase 0 pilot analysis and RIP framework. Exec-only training amplifies hacking from 25% (base model) to 45% because GRPO's reward gradient selects for outputs that prove easily, and structural shortcuts prove more easily than genuine formalizations. The distribution is dominated by tautological premises (18%) and vacuous quantifiers (12%) — the two cheapest hacking modes (highest reward-per-character). Exec+STA training suppresses hacking to 18% (below base model's 25%) because STA penalizes these modes: tautological premises drop from 18%→3% (STA assigns ISR=0 to tautology components), vacuous quantifiers from 12%→4%. Residual hacking (18%) consists of subtler shortcuts that partial ISR doesn't fully penalize — primarily redundant clauses (6%) that have small but nonzero ISR contributions. SFT's low hack rate (12%) sets the floor — human-written outputs have few structural shortcuts. Inter-annotator agreement κ=0.78 (substantial) is achievable because the taxonomy categories have operational definitions tied to STA component ablation patterns.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":767,"startLine":1,"totalLines":767}}}
